1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,760
The most powerful boat hoist.

2
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:05,400
The hungriest car shredder.

3
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:09,980
There's just so much chaos and abuse in this machine.

4
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:12,480
They parachute the heaviest cargo.

5
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:16,720
They smash the most cars.

6
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:19,420
It's a truck on steroids. It's a huge truck.

7
00:00:19,580 --> 00:00:21,300
From the ultimate bulletproof vest

8
00:00:21,301 --> 00:00:24,960
to a pogo stick engineered for totally awesome heights.

9
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:27,360
They are the world's strongest.

10
00:00:27,361 --> 00:00:29,360
Now on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Is there a limit to how strong something can be?

12
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:50,360
Science and industry's demand for strength is insatiable.

13
00:00:50,361 --> 00:00:53,600
And technological breakthroughs constantly push the envelope of

14
00:00:53,601 --> 00:00:57,600
what's considered the most powerful, creating new claims

15
00:00:57,601 --> 00:01:00,000
to the title of the world's strongest.

16
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:08,640
Truckloads of junked cars and scrap metal

17
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:12,940
are arriving at Sturges Iron and Metal in Elkhart, Indiana.

18
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:15,360
Powerful cranes unload the trucks,

19
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:18,960
stacking the scrap into enormous piles.

20
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:22,060
But the piles won't be there long.

21
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,460
They're headed for the jaws of the heavy shredder.

22
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:30,900
the largest and most powerful metal shredder in the world.

23
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:35,740
Cranes feeding this metal-eating monster

24
00:01:35,741 --> 00:01:38,680
can barely keep up with its appetite for deconstruction.

25
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:42,880
It can consume up to 480 cars an hour,

26
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:46,040
four times the output of conventional shredders,

27
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:50,900
reducing vehicles to fist-sized pieces of twisted scrap.

28
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:54,700
The world's growing demand for recycled steel

29
00:01:54,701 --> 00:01:56,600
has created a need for this colossus.

30
00:01:56,601 --> 00:01:59,800
Well, what we've got is an 8,000 horsepower

31
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:01,740
oversized paper shredder.

32
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:04,680
And it's fed by a conveyor that's effectively

33
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:08,580
a 200-foot-long, 8-foot-wide bulldozer track.

34
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:11,220
Takes the car bodies up.

35
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:13,180
They go down into the shredder, and like your mother's

36
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:15,280
old ringer washer, there's a set of rollers that

37
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:17,460
push the cars into the shredder.

38
00:02:17,560 --> 00:02:19,720
It is all shredded down to small pieces

39
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:23,960
through grates in the bottom like an oversized cheese grater.

40
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:26,200
Inside the machine is a giant rotor

41
00:02:26,201 --> 00:02:29,700
that's mounted with two dozen 1,200-pound hammers,

42
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:33,840
rotating at 450 revolutions per minute.

43
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:37,980
The hammers slam into the scrap at 175 miles per hour,

44
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:42,220
pulverizing entire cars into tiny pieces.

45
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:44,680
Mike Woodward, who helped design this shredder,

46
00:02:44,780 --> 00:02:47,180
offers a look under the hood.

47
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:50,320
The shredder rotates in this direction,

48
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:52,720
and the cars come down the feed chute right here,

49
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:55,460
underneath the feed roller.

50
00:02:55,461 --> 00:03:04,013
And against the anvil, the spreader bar, and the cutter bar, the hammers, which are whipped out with centrifugal force, come down and they strike the car, chopping it

51
00:03:04,014 --> 00:03:04,940
into small pieces.

52
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:10,300
The machine itself, we've got four-inch thick walls and ten-inch thick liners.

53
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:15,080
There's just so much chaos and abuse in this machine.

54
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:17,500
It has to take an unbelievable amount of pounding.

55
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:22,780
The shredder's rotating hammers receive nearly as much of a pounding as they deliver.

56
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:25,820
This hammer's been used once on this edge.

57
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:27,620
You can compare it right here to this hammer

58
00:03:27,621 --> 00:03:29,140
that hasn't had this edge worn yet.

59
00:03:30,940 --> 00:03:34,280
The sheer volume this shredding giant consumes is mind-numbing.

60
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:36,660
We're talking six to eight cars a minute.

61
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:38,740
So you can imagine the logistics

62
00:03:38,741 --> 00:03:40,920
just getting that material to the shredder.

63
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:42,700
The number of trucks that got to move,

64
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:44,920
the number of crane operators that have to be doing their thing just right,

65
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:47,180
getting them onto that infy conveyor, all stacked.

66
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,380
It's a ballet, literally.

67
00:03:49,381 --> 00:03:52,380
The choreographer is the machine operator,

68
00:03:52,381 --> 00:03:56,380
who oversees the workflow from a perch just above the mouth of the shredder.

69
00:03:56,381 --> 00:04:01,380
We run everything, all the belts and feed, shredder,

70
00:04:01,381 --> 00:04:04,380
from right here in this area you see right here.

71
00:04:04,381 --> 00:04:08,380
My job is to control the material going into the shredder.

72
00:04:08,381 --> 00:04:12,380
One hand control operates the belts feeding into the shredder.

73
00:04:12,381 --> 00:04:16,380
The other raises and lowers the feed rollers

74
00:04:16,381 --> 00:04:20,380
to accommodate pieces of different size.

75
00:04:20,381 --> 00:04:22,880
Computer screens allow him to monitor pumps,

76
00:04:22,881 --> 00:04:25,380
bearing pressures, rotor temperatures,

77
00:04:25,381 --> 00:04:28,380
or any delays that might have occurred downstream.

78
00:04:28,381 --> 00:04:30,380
There's a lot of automation in the controls,

79
00:04:30,381 --> 00:04:33,380
but nothing can do as good a job as a man's eyes and hands

80
00:04:33,381 --> 00:04:36,380
for some of the oddballs that might be in the chute.

81
00:04:36,381 --> 00:04:39,380
We are going to run a school bus here in a second.

82
00:04:39,381 --> 00:04:42,380
Even a bus is no challenge for this behemoth.

83
00:04:42,381 --> 00:04:47,880
The feeder belt that pulls heavy loads up this steep incline

84
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:51,880
produces a force equal to five D5 Caterpillar tractors.

85
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:08,940
The shredder devours the school bus with ease.

86
00:05:08,941 --> 00:05:12,380
with ease, thanks to its 8,000 horsepower wound rotor

87
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:14,440
electric motor.

88
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:18,380
That's the equivalent of four locomotive engines.

89
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:23,280
It runs on 4,160 volts and is fitted with a large liquid

90
00:05:23,380 --> 00:05:26,600
rheostat, which functions like a giant dimmer switch to prevent

91
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:30,100
the motor from drawing too much current.

92
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:32,000
If it didn't have a liquid rheostat,

93
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:36,400
if it stalled out, it could brown out the city.

94
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:38,400
Just as impressive is the size and power

95
00:05:38,401 --> 00:05:41,040
of this new shredding technology is the thoroughness

96
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:44,180
of its recycling capacity.

97
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:46,400
400 tons of shredded scrap per hour

98
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:48,440
move along conveyor belts where magnets

99
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:51,720
extract the steel elements.

100
00:05:51,820 --> 00:05:53,180
There's a series of inspectors who

101
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:55,120
monitor the scrap as it goes by, and they

102
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:56,460
look for things that shouldn't be there.

103
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:57,780
Copper is a big one.

104
00:05:57,880 --> 00:05:58,880
Copper is bad for steel.

105
00:05:58,980 --> 00:06:00,960
It's the kind of thing that sunk the Titanic.

106
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:03,760
If there's copper in your steel, it can make it brittle.

107
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:05,400
When it comes out the end, we call it frag,

108
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:07,700
and it's clean steel, ready for the steel mill.

109
00:06:07,701 --> 00:06:09,060
to melt down and make new steel.

110
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:14,400
All the other material is what we call the fluff

111
00:06:14,401 --> 00:06:15,800
or auto shredder residue.

112
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:18,980
What we have here is the material that's been screened

113
00:06:18,981 --> 00:06:20,640
through about a one and a quarter inch screen.

114
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,280
It has no metals left in it.

115
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:23,840
This is now what has to go to the landfill.

116
00:06:26,160 --> 00:06:28,620
Sturgis Iron and Metal maintains its own railroad

117
00:06:28,621 --> 00:06:30,440
to deliver frag to the steel mill.

118
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:34,200
But the heavy shredder has such an enormous output,

119
00:06:34,760 --> 00:06:36,060
sometimes it's hard to keep up.

120
00:06:36,061 --> 00:06:39,700
Productivity of the machine is such that we actually run out

121
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:41,460
of rail cars throughout the day.

122
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:44,000
Sometimes they'll bring us 15, 20 cars.

123
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:47,500
It's not enough for the day.

124
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:50,500
Being that productive takes a toll on the shredder,

125
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:54,920
especially in areas of metal-on-metal contact.

126
00:06:55,020 --> 00:06:57,920
Daily service is required for the replaceable wear parts

127
00:06:58,020 --> 00:06:59,520
on the shredder's rotor.

128
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:01,560
Every night, they have to change hammers like this,

129
00:07:01,660 --> 00:07:03,460
turn them around, and reorganize them

130
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:05,460
for maximum productivity.

131
00:07:05,461 --> 00:07:10,560
The introduction of high-efficiency, high-volume

132
00:07:10,561 --> 00:07:14,340
shredding giants will ultimately reduce the number of scrap metal

133
00:07:14,341 --> 00:07:17,440
processing facilities necessary for recycling purposes.

134
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:24,380
But recycling on a scale like this comes with a price.

135
00:07:24,381 --> 00:07:25,980
Noise and vibration from the shredder

136
00:07:25,981 --> 00:07:30,580
affect the nearby community, creating tensions.

137
00:07:30,581 --> 00:07:32,660
The shredder's owners are involved in efforts

138
00:07:32,661 --> 00:07:36,160
to reduce the machine's impact on its neighbors.

139
00:07:36,161 --> 00:07:39,540
But the expression, one man's trash is another man's treasure,

140
00:07:39,541 --> 00:07:42,220
has never been truer, as the world's hunger

141
00:07:42,221 --> 00:07:46,600
for secondhand steel increases.

142
00:07:46,601 --> 00:07:49,140
Another of the world's strongest demonstrates power

143
00:07:49,141 --> 00:07:51,140
of a different sort.

144
00:07:51,141 --> 00:07:53,920
High over the Yuma Proving Grounds in Arizona,

145
00:07:53,921 --> 00:07:57,760
a C-17 aircraft cruises above the desert at 20,000 feet.

146
00:07:57,761 --> 00:08:02,760
Over 25,000 pounds of cargo ejects from the rear door,

147
00:08:02,860 --> 00:08:04,760
and a massive parachute deploys,

148
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:07,260
billowing out like a silk cloud.

149
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:11,100
It's the strongest, largest ram air parachute in the world,

150
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:15,220
and this is the heaviest cargo it has ever tried to carry.

151
00:08:16,520 --> 00:08:19,720
It's been dubbed MegaFly.

152
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:22,520
A ram air parachute is an airfoil,

153
00:08:22,620 --> 00:08:24,560
just like an airplane wing.

154
00:08:24,561 --> 00:08:27,600
is an airfoil, just like an airplane wing,

155
00:08:27,700 --> 00:08:29,600
that maintains its shape by trapping air

156
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:32,160
between two rectangular sheets sewn together

157
00:08:32,260 --> 00:08:35,840
at the trailing edge and sides, but open at the front.

158
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:38,780
A round parachute only provides drag.

159
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:41,380
Basically, the bigger the parachute,

160
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:44,180
the slower it will come down.

161
00:08:44,280 --> 00:08:47,520
A ramia parachute provides lift.

162
00:08:47,620 --> 00:08:49,780
And therefore, if it moves across the sky,

163
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:51,920
then it will allow to carry the weight.

164
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:57,800
The strongest round parachutes are those used to recover

165
00:08:57,900 --> 00:09:01,100
solid rocket boosters deployed in NASA's space shuttle launches.

166
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:08,100
They're 136 feet in diameter and can bear weights

167
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:11,920
up to 195,000 pounds.

168
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:16,620
By contrast, Megafly is 170 feet from wingtip to wingtip,

169
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:19,360
56 feet from back to front.

170
00:09:19,460 --> 00:09:22,060
It's 9,000 square feet in diameter,

171
00:09:22,061 --> 00:09:24,360
or one quarter the size of a football field.

172
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:28,100
Though round parachutes may be able to carry heavier loads,

173
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:30,200
they offer little or no control.

174
00:09:31,340 --> 00:09:34,060
When you drop out of an aircraft with round parachutes,

175
00:09:34,061 --> 00:09:39,000
it could land 20 to 40 kilometers off from your target due to the winds.

176
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:43,180
So the Megafly provides the capability of precision

177
00:09:43,181 --> 00:09:46,460
so it can steer to the location that you want.

178
00:09:46,461 --> 00:09:54,740
Megafly's precision comes from an onboard programmable GPS system that allows it to

179
00:09:54,741 --> 00:09:56,980
fly autonomously to designated drop zones.

180
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:00,160
It weighs 350 pounds.

181
00:10:00,580 --> 00:10:04,620
140 pounds of it is just batteries to control the system.

182
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:09,660
It can hit a target the size of a soccer field from a distance of 20 kilometers.

183
00:10:10,980 --> 00:10:13,580
Note how close Megafly comes to its drop zone marker.

184
00:10:16,460 --> 00:10:19,520
The first item that we have here is the GPS receiver,

185
00:10:19,940 --> 00:10:24,300
which tells us where we are in the sky and guides us to our location.

186
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:27,620
What it does is it controls two 11-horsepower motors.

187
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:29,900
We have one on this side and one on the other side,

188
00:10:30,300 --> 00:10:34,060
which control the guidelines and steer the parafoil.

189
00:10:37,220 --> 00:10:40,800
The demand for an accurate heavy payload aerial delivery system

190
00:10:40,801 --> 00:10:42,540
led the Army to develop Megafly.

191
00:10:42,541 --> 00:10:49,380
The biggest benefit of MegaFly is that we'll no longer have to have troops and convoys

192
00:10:49,381 --> 00:10:52,060
delivering supplies to the soldiers out in the field.

193
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:56,780
We'll be able to fly all of the supplies directly to the soldiers

194
00:10:56,781 --> 00:10:59,460
so we don't have to worry about anyone being harmed on the ground.

195
00:11:01,700 --> 00:11:04,800
Ram Air parachutes were introduced in the mid-1960s

196
00:11:04,801 --> 00:11:08,160
and quickly displaced round parachutes for recreational use.

197
00:11:08,161 --> 00:11:15,660
Creating a ram air parachute strong enough to bear 30,000 pounds

198
00:11:15,661 --> 00:11:18,960
required much more than simply scaling up the size of the chute.

199
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:24,580
We had to depart from the current concept of making only one parachute.

200
00:11:25,060 --> 00:11:30,340
So we had to make this parachute in sections that would be manufactured separately

201
00:11:30,341 --> 00:11:33,620
and then put together in order to create this big parachute.

202
00:11:34,240 --> 00:11:38,080
This is a full model of Megafly, as you would see it in the air,

203
00:11:38,081 --> 00:11:39,120
when it's fully inflated.

204
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:42,420
As you can see, the parachute is constructed in five sections

205
00:11:42,421 --> 00:11:45,460
and five miles of suspension lines.

206
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:49,580
The designs JC Berland creates in his computer

207
00:11:49,581 --> 00:11:52,260
are sent directly to the laser cutter on the factory floor.

208
00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:56,200
The benefit is cost savings, time saving,

209
00:11:56,520 --> 00:11:59,600
and complete and total accuracy for making our designs.

210
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:01,380
Depending upon the pattern,

211
00:12:01,381 --> 00:12:05,120
this machine will cut up to roughly two parachutes every hour.

212
00:12:05,121 --> 00:12:08,260
In the old days, that would take probably a day and a half

213
00:12:08,261 --> 00:12:09,280
worth of manual cutting.

214
00:12:10,980 --> 00:12:13,580
Reinforcement tape is sewn into the ribs by hand.

215
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:18,980
Technicians rigorously test fabric and cables at strength

216
00:12:18,981 --> 00:12:22,080
levels far exceeding the required 30,000-pound tolerance.

217
00:12:24,140 --> 00:12:28,160
Megafly's five miles of line tests at 60,000 pounds.

218
00:12:29,640 --> 00:12:31,960
Each piece is cut to a precise length

219
00:12:31,961 --> 00:12:33,600
to maintain the shape of the parachute

220
00:12:33,601 --> 00:12:36,460
and distribute the weight evenly before it's ready to fly.

221
00:12:37,900 --> 00:12:42,660
It takes approximately one full day to pack this entire parachute.

222
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:47,320
It is then assembled onto the autopilot system,

223
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:52,960
and this entire system is then placed on top of the payload and secured.

224
00:12:53,740 --> 00:12:55,960
To launch Megafly from an aircraft,

225
00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:58,900
first a small extraction parachute deploys,

226
00:12:59,460 --> 00:13:00,860
propelling the cargo from the plane.

227
00:13:00,861 --> 00:13:08,660
The force of the extraction parachute pulls the giant parafoil off of the payload, causing it to billow open.

228
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,480
You see basically a huge wing coming to life.

229
00:13:13,140 --> 00:13:14,900
It is the largest parachute in the world.

230
00:13:15,980 --> 00:13:18,200
It's immense. I mean, it covers a lot of sky.

231
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:22,080
The size of the chute is what offsets the weight of the load,

232
00:13:22,460 --> 00:13:26,600
with each of its 9,000 square feet supporting just two to three pounds,

233
00:13:27,380 --> 00:13:30,260
comparable to the weight disbursement of a standard personal parachute.

234
00:13:30,860 --> 00:13:36,860
Ground recovery of the parachute is simple, since each of its five panels separates.

235
00:13:36,861 --> 00:13:41,860
Depending on the terrain, Megafly can be gathered up in less than an hour.

236
00:13:41,861 --> 00:13:45,860
These five bins contain the five segments of the 30K Megafly.

237
00:13:45,861 --> 00:13:51,860
After each drop, the system is collected, recovered, and returned for inspection, maintenance, and repair.

238
00:13:51,861 --> 00:13:58,860
Size comparison, this is a 380-square-foot personnel parachute, as compared with the 9,000-square-foot Megafly.

239
00:13:58,861 --> 00:14:01,760
foot mega fly.

240
00:14:01,860 --> 00:14:06,260
The future of autopiloted parachutes is big and heavy.

241
00:14:06,360 --> 00:14:10,000
Four years ago, we thought we would never get 10,000 pounds.

242
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:11,800
And now we're at 30.

243
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:14,000
I don't think there's really a limit to the technology

244
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:16,720
that we can clearly see at this point.

245
00:14:16,820 --> 00:14:20,220
This precision guided flying machine, such as mega flight,

246
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:23,060
are really the beginning of a new era.

247
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:25,160
Lifting power like mega flies is one

248
00:14:25,260 --> 00:14:27,960
sure measure of strength.

249
00:14:27,961 --> 00:14:30,160
So is the car-crushing force of this giant.

250
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:35,160
But it also shows its strength in some other very surprising ways.

251
00:14:36,460 --> 00:14:40,540
Megafly's ability to hit its drop zone from 5 miles up and 19 miles away

252
00:14:40,541 --> 00:14:44,740
is considered to be as difficult as hitting a 2-inch target from 1,400 feet away.

253
00:14:45,080 --> 00:14:48,060
World's Strongest 2 will return on Modern Marvels.

254
00:14:57,960 --> 00:15:03,300
In the realm of 20,000-pound, high-flying, car-crushing,

255
00:15:03,301 --> 00:15:06,100
1,500-horsepower Monster Trucks,

256
00:15:06,460 --> 00:15:10,980
one icon stands above all others, Bigfoot.

257
00:15:11,980 --> 00:15:14,900
It has to be strong to stand up to abuse like this.

258
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:26,800
The will of Monster Trucks' strength is,

259
00:15:26,801 --> 00:15:30,640
to me the ability to have that truck last through a race

260
00:15:30,641 --> 00:15:32,460
and through the exhibitions and drive away

261
00:15:32,461 --> 00:15:34,180
after the show's over in one piece.

262
00:15:36,260 --> 00:15:38,460
Bigfoot has done more than just survive.

263
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:41,560
In its various incarnations, it has dominated

264
00:15:41,561 --> 00:15:43,060
the sport of monster trucks.

265
00:15:44,080 --> 00:15:46,760
It's been taller and heavier, 15 and 1

266
00:15:46,761 --> 00:15:48,680
feet and 28,000 pounds.

267
00:15:49,360 --> 00:15:51,820
It's jumped higher, 24 feet.

268
00:15:52,740 --> 00:15:56,780
It's performed the longest recorded wheelie, 217.

269
00:15:56,800 --> 00:16:01,600
It's won 20 championships, and it set the record for both speed

270
00:16:01,700 --> 00:16:07,140
and length of jump when it vaulted a 727 airliner in 1999

271
00:16:07,240 --> 00:16:12,480
at 69 miles per hour and 202 feet.

272
00:16:12,580 --> 00:16:16,080
This monster's strength has evolved.

273
00:16:16,180 --> 00:16:19,280
When you talk about Bigfoot, you're really talking about 15

274
00:16:19,380 --> 00:16:23,420
generations of trucks operating under that banner, all designed

275
00:16:23,421 --> 00:16:25,560
all designed and built by Bob Chandler,

276
00:16:25,860 --> 00:16:29,060
who created the first monster truck back in 1975.

277
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:31,200
I didn't sit in the back of my mind and say,

278
00:16:31,260 --> 00:16:32,120
I want to build a monster truck.

279
00:16:32,200 --> 00:16:34,960
It's just something that happened over a two- or three-year period.

280
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:39,740
Bob operated a 4x4 garage just outside St. Louis, Missouri.

281
00:16:40,600 --> 00:16:44,180
He used his own truck as a showcase for equipment he wanted to sell.

282
00:16:44,700 --> 00:16:45,920
This is Bigfoot number one.

283
00:16:45,921 --> 00:16:50,140
This was my truck I bought new, 1974 F-250 Ford,

284
00:16:50,580 --> 00:16:52,900
drove it back to my garage and started changing things on it.

285
00:16:52,901 --> 00:16:56,280
Probably the first truck that had this big tire on,

286
00:16:56,281 --> 00:16:57,480
this Terra tire on it.

287
00:16:57,481 --> 00:17:00,740
Big tires called for a stronger axle.

288
00:17:00,741 --> 00:17:03,580
A stronger axle called for a bigger engine.

289
00:17:03,581 --> 00:17:07,280
The added weight called for a stronger frame and suspension.

290
00:17:07,281 --> 00:17:09,880
So it was a vicious cycle till it got to the point

291
00:17:09,881 --> 00:17:12,080
where I run these 66-inch tall tires.

292
00:17:12,081 --> 00:17:15,260
My general manager, Ron Magruder, started calling me Bigfoot

293
00:17:15,261 --> 00:17:17,260
because I couldn't keep my foot out of the throttle.

294
00:17:17,261 --> 00:17:19,620
And I liked the name, so I stuck it on the side of the truck.

295
00:17:19,621 --> 00:17:25,940
Bigfoot was so much stronger than its 4x4 competitors that it soon had many imitators,

296
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:30,380
which ultimately led to monster trucking becoming a genuine motorsport.

297
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:36,180
Even drivers that race against Bigfoot recognize Chandler's contributions.

298
00:17:37,060 --> 00:17:38,860
Bob Chandler pretty much started it all.

299
00:17:38,861 --> 00:17:45,020
I mean, if Chandler didn't crush that first car, I wouldn't be standing here talking to you guys right now.

300
00:17:45,160 --> 00:17:48,000
Without him, I wouldn't have a job, you know, and I've looked up to him for a lot of years.

301
00:17:48,001 --> 00:17:52,080
Anytime we race the Bigfoot team, that's our big competition.

302
00:17:53,900 --> 00:17:59,220
These skilled drivers race these giants over jumps at speeds approaching 70 miles per hour

303
00:17:59,221 --> 00:18:04,860
and perform radical freestyle exhibitions to test the vehicle's structural limits.

304
00:18:06,040 --> 00:18:10,100
To make it strong, the frame has to be strong, the axles have to be strong,

305
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:12,820
the engine, the transmission, the whole vehicle has to be strong.

306
00:18:12,821 --> 00:18:18,920
My concern right now is frames, because we're having a lot of frame fatigue problems over the years.

307
00:18:19,420 --> 00:18:21,480
So I'm designing a new frame now.

308
00:18:22,840 --> 00:18:26,880
Bob uses carefully arranged steel tubing to make Bigfoot's frame.

309
00:18:27,460 --> 00:18:32,660
It's definitely the geometry that makes it stronger, not the amount of tubes you put in it.

310
00:18:34,020 --> 00:18:39,220
Bob designs his trucks using AutoCAD and the latest finite element analysis program

311
00:18:39,221 --> 00:18:42,380
to help him distribute stress points evenly throughout the vehicle.

312
00:18:42,820 --> 00:18:47,860
What we're looking at is a new drawing that we're making that I'm working on for our Bigfoot.

313
00:18:47,960 --> 00:18:51,160
And if you can see here, it starts out with just a basic frame rail.

314
00:18:51,161 --> 00:18:55,360
And then we add the cage, the driver, subframe.

315
00:18:55,800 --> 00:18:57,420
And it's just basically putting pieces together.

316
00:18:57,600 --> 00:19:01,060
And I'm configuring them so the truck's going to be stronger overall.

317
00:19:02,280 --> 00:19:07,380
Bob brings his designs to life in his 24,000-square-foot garage in Hazelwood, Missouri.

318
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:09,920
This is one of the current chassis we use right now.

319
00:19:10,100 --> 00:19:11,300
Chassis kind of in construction.

320
00:19:11,301 --> 00:19:12,200
You can see this.

321
00:19:12,640 --> 00:19:14,060
This is the main frame right here.

322
00:19:14,400 --> 00:19:17,620
We run a nitrogen gas shock, which is so much stronger than our leaf springs.

323
00:19:17,660 --> 00:19:20,300
It doesn't appear to be the stronger, but it absorbs some more.

324
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:21,060
It's more energy.

325
00:19:21,220 --> 00:19:22,960
It cushions the ride for the drivers.

326
00:19:24,840 --> 00:19:30,440
These shocks allow 25 to 30 inches of suspension travel to help absorb the impact of launching

327
00:19:30,441 --> 00:19:33,420
20,000 pounds of truck 20 feet in the air.

328
00:19:34,620 --> 00:19:35,740
This is the driver's compartment.

329
00:19:35,860 --> 00:19:38,200
You'll see there's a lot of Lexan in here, so the driver can see.

330
00:19:38,201 --> 00:19:40,480
He sets so high, so he needs good visibility,

331
00:19:40,680 --> 00:19:42,280
so he can see out the back, he can see out the front,

332
00:19:42,320 --> 00:19:44,220
he can see out underneath the truck when he's sitting in the seat.

333
00:19:46,020 --> 00:19:48,160
Visibility through the truck's floor comes in handy

334
00:19:48,161 --> 00:19:50,040
when popping wheelies the length of a stadium.

335
00:19:51,180 --> 00:19:52,800
These are the axles. They're made in Germany.

336
00:19:52,920 --> 00:19:54,120
ZF axles is what they're called.

337
00:19:54,440 --> 00:19:57,700
And what makes them so strong is the planetary is right here at the wheel hub,

338
00:19:57,880 --> 00:20:01,800
so it transfers all the power going to it is reduced right here.

339
00:20:03,420 --> 00:20:06,040
On early monster trucks, as on most vehicles,

340
00:20:06,800 --> 00:20:10,260
Gear reduction, the transfer of energy from the driveshaft to the axle,

341
00:20:10,820 --> 00:20:12,960
occurred in the transmission and the transfer case,

342
00:20:13,260 --> 00:20:16,280
which added great weight and stress to the vehicle's rear axle.

343
00:20:17,960 --> 00:20:21,420
But a planetary gear set can be mounted right at the hub of the axle.

344
00:20:22,960 --> 00:20:25,480
A planetary, often used in heavy equipment,

345
00:20:26,060 --> 00:20:29,860
is a gear set in which all of the gears are grouped around each other on a single plane,

346
00:20:30,340 --> 00:20:31,900
like the planets around the sun.

347
00:20:32,620 --> 00:20:35,380
The gear in the center is called the sun gear.

348
00:20:35,381 --> 00:20:38,460
It meshes with the gears that surround it, called planet gears,

349
00:20:38,860 --> 00:20:41,280
which are attached to the wheels, causing them to turn.

350
00:20:43,080 --> 00:20:48,620
This enables Bigfoot to run on giant 66-inch tires with a small, relatively light axle.

351
00:20:49,600 --> 00:20:53,940
The strength is put all the way at the wheel rather than through the whole drivetrain.

352
00:20:54,440 --> 00:20:55,660
That really worked well.

353
00:20:55,740 --> 00:20:58,220
That's the only thing that probably saved our industry.

354
00:20:58,960 --> 00:21:03,380
The use of planetaries was just one of many innovations that Bob introduced to the sport.

355
00:21:03,381 --> 00:21:07,380
Lightening the truck's tire weight was another breakthrough.

356
00:21:07,381 --> 00:21:10,380
Bob and his team cut excess bulk rubber off the tire

357
00:21:10,381 --> 00:21:12,380
and customized the tread.

358
00:21:12,381 --> 00:21:15,380
It's been said that taking 10 pounds of weight off the tire

359
00:21:15,381 --> 00:21:17,380
is like taking 100 pounds off the car.

360
00:21:17,381 --> 00:21:19,380
And we take as much as 400 pounds off the tire,

361
00:21:19,381 --> 00:21:21,380
so that just makes our truck faster

362
00:21:21,381 --> 00:21:23,380
because it spins the tires quicker.

363
00:21:25,380 --> 00:21:29,380
When its tires spin, Bigfoot gets to show off its real strength.

364
00:21:29,381 --> 00:21:32,380
Whether it's the power of its acceleration,

365
00:21:32,381 --> 00:21:37,380
the sturdiness of its axles, or the ruggedness of its frame.

366
00:21:37,480 --> 00:21:40,860
Bigfoot's ability to withstand punishment also plays a role

367
00:21:40,960 --> 00:21:43,520
in the safety of its drivers.

368
00:21:43,620 --> 00:21:45,920
We hit a rollover where I actually

369
00:21:46,020 --> 00:21:49,100
rolled one at 60 or 65 mile an hour

370
00:21:49,200 --> 00:21:50,500
at the end of a racetrack.

371
00:21:50,600 --> 00:21:53,500
And the only thing that was left bolted on the truck body

372
00:21:53,600 --> 00:21:54,800
wise was the cab.

373
00:21:54,900 --> 00:21:56,240
And I mean, it was destroyed.

374
00:21:56,340 --> 00:21:59,500
The roll cage held up.

375
00:21:59,600 --> 00:22:02,380
Bob Chandler builds his trucks with a single

376
00:22:02,381 --> 00:22:05,920
a safety factor of 20, meaning that if a part is expected

377
00:22:06,020 --> 00:22:08,980
to break at a certain pressure, he builds it 20 times

378
00:22:09,080 --> 00:22:10,720
that strength.

379
00:22:10,820 --> 00:22:13,820
He plans for his next Bigfoot model to be even stronger.

380
00:22:13,920 --> 00:22:17,420
Now I've got to rotate this thing over and actually land

381
00:22:17,520 --> 00:22:19,600
on its roof and put pressure from the top

382
00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:22,140
and see how strong it will be on a rollover.

383
00:22:22,240 --> 00:22:25,240
I've got to go through probably 100 different configurations

384
00:22:25,340 --> 00:22:29,340
before I can see if this is going to be strong enough.

385
00:22:29,440 --> 00:22:31,180
Chandler learns as much from his failures

386
00:22:31,181 --> 00:22:32,140
he does from his victories.

387
00:22:35,980 --> 00:22:39,100
And the evolution of Bigfoot seems poised to continue.

388
00:22:40,400 --> 00:22:43,060
30 years and 15 generations of design

389
00:22:43,061 --> 00:22:46,980
have raised Bigfoot to the top of the heap of monster trucks.

390
00:22:49,320 --> 00:22:52,360
Raising a 650-ton mega yacht, however,

391
00:22:52,700 --> 00:22:55,940
requires the power of the world's strongest boat hoist.

392
00:22:57,500 --> 00:22:59,820
There is no Bigfoot number 13,

393
00:22:59,821 --> 00:23:03,660
since the number was considered unlucky by Team Bigfoot.

394
00:23:03,760 --> 00:23:08,860
World's Strongest Two will return on Modern Marvels.

395
00:23:08,960 --> 00:23:10,960
Mario Olivares is at the controls

396
00:23:11,060 --> 00:23:13,900
of a new piece of super strong equipment

397
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:17,800
at the Marine Group Boat Works in San Diego, California.

398
00:23:17,900 --> 00:23:20,040
It's like a remote control car.

399
00:23:20,140 --> 00:23:22,480
Same thing, just more buttons.

400
00:23:22,580 --> 00:23:24,380
Just with a lot of responsibilities.

401
00:23:26,380 --> 00:23:28,340
Mario is piloting the latest model

402
00:23:28,341 --> 00:23:32,580
model marine travel lift, one of only three in the world.

403
00:23:32,581 --> 00:23:33,680
Its cargo?

404
00:23:33,681 --> 00:23:39,580
The California Spirit, a 440-ton charter yacht that can accommodate

405
00:23:39,581 --> 00:23:41,980
600 passengers.

406
00:23:41,981 --> 00:23:44,900
It barely taxes the capacity of the world's strongest mobile

407
00:23:44,901 --> 00:23:51,100
yacht hoist that not only lifts, but also transports its cargo.

408
00:23:51,101 --> 00:23:54,000
The biggest boat we've lifted in terms of length would be a

409
00:23:54,001 --> 00:23:57,900
private yacht named Ronin, which was 193 feet long.

410
00:23:57,901 --> 00:24:03,740
owned by larry ellison a founder of oracle that both probably were somewhere in the 40 to 60 million

411
00:24:03,741 --> 00:24:11,420
dollar range whether you call them super yachts mega yachts or giga yachts oversized recreational

412
00:24:11,421 --> 00:24:18,140
boats are the latest trend this year alone there's 600 yachts over 150 feet currently under

413
00:24:18,141 --> 00:24:22,860
construction worldwide 10 years ago these boats didn't exist this market didn't exist

414
00:24:22,861 --> 00:24:27,860
With outsized boats came the need for an outsized pick-me-up.

415
00:24:27,861 --> 00:24:31,860
The Marina Boatworks keeps three different sized lifts on hand.

416
00:24:31,861 --> 00:24:35,860
The smallest can handle boats up to 25 tons.

417
00:24:35,861 --> 00:24:39,860
The midsize is capable of lifting 100 tons.

418
00:24:39,861 --> 00:24:45,860
The largest and strongest hoist handles boats up to 665 tons,

419
00:24:45,861 --> 00:24:49,860
moving them around the shipyard like bathtub toys.

420
00:24:49,861 --> 00:24:52,600
like bathtub toys.

421
00:24:52,601 --> 00:24:56,180
So how did it get the California spirit up in the air and moving?

422
00:24:56,181 --> 00:24:58,220
It's actually as simple as it looks.

423
00:24:58,221 --> 00:25:01,160
The machine drives out on the finger piers.

424
00:25:01,161 --> 00:25:03,560
We lower the straps down into the water deep enough

425
00:25:03,561 --> 00:25:05,440
such that the boat can drive in.

426
00:25:05,441 --> 00:25:08,100
We then adjust our straps to a point

427
00:25:08,101 --> 00:25:10,140
where we know it's safe to lift the boat,

428
00:25:10,141 --> 00:25:12,580
and then we hoist away on the straps.

429
00:25:12,581 --> 00:25:13,281
Here we go.

430
00:25:17,860 --> 00:25:19,340
The straps are pulled into position

431
00:25:19,341 --> 00:25:22,880
by over 1,000 feet of inch-and-a-half galvanized steel

432
00:25:22,881 --> 00:25:26,840
triple-braided cables, which hoist the boat at 7 inches

433
00:25:26,841 --> 00:25:29,240
per minute.

434
00:25:29,241 --> 00:25:32,120
The machine's lifting strength comes from hydraulics,

435
00:25:32,121 --> 00:25:34,120
not horsepower.

436
00:25:34,121 --> 00:25:35,120
The motor is very simple.

437
00:25:35,121 --> 00:25:38,520
It's a 400-horsepower turbocharged John Deere,

438
00:25:38,521 --> 00:25:40,580
which runs a large hydraulic pump.

439
00:25:40,581 --> 00:25:43,000
And then all the motors that control the winches, the wheels,

440
00:25:43,001 --> 00:25:46,380
the steering, everything's hydraulic.

441
00:25:46,381 --> 00:25:49,880
The engine turns at 1,800 revolutions per minute,

442
00:25:49,881 --> 00:25:52,400
pumping hydraulic fluid to a series of valves,

443
00:25:52,401 --> 00:25:54,600
which control where the fluid is directed.

444
00:25:54,601 --> 00:25:57,480
When the hoist valve is open, the flow of hydraulic fluid

445
00:25:57,481 --> 00:25:59,880
is forced through a system of stainless steel tubing

446
00:25:59,881 --> 00:26:04,600
to the winch, which it turns, lifting the boat.

447
00:26:04,601 --> 00:26:06,560
Once the boat is aloft, the operator

448
00:26:06,561 --> 00:26:10,660
guides the marine travel lift at one mile per hour.

449
00:26:10,661 --> 00:26:12,280
The wireless remote control allows

450
00:26:12,281 --> 00:26:14,400
him to steer the massive front and rear wheels

451
00:26:14,401 --> 00:26:17,140
independently.

452
00:26:17,141 --> 00:26:18,380
They're 16 wheels.

453
00:26:18,381 --> 00:26:20,400
They're approximately 9 feet tall.

454
00:26:20,401 --> 00:26:23,900
They run at about 140 PSI in comparison to a car that runs

455
00:26:23,901 --> 00:26:26,220
at about 35 PSI.

456
00:26:26,221 --> 00:26:28,920
And they're capable of bearing the weight of both the machine

457
00:26:28,921 --> 00:26:31,280
itself, which is about a million pounds,

458
00:26:31,281 --> 00:26:33,360
and then the weight of its cargo, which can be upwards

459
00:26:33,361 --> 00:26:37,320
of 1.3 million pounds.

460
00:26:37,321 --> 00:26:40,060
Wheeling a large craft around a boatyard for maintenance

461
00:26:40,061 --> 00:26:42,400
isn't a new idea.

462
00:26:42,401 --> 00:26:46,060
The marine travel lift first appeared in boat yards in 1958

463
00:26:46,061 --> 00:26:49,240
with a self-propelled hoist that could lift a whopping seven

464
00:26:49,241 --> 00:26:51,080
tons.

465
00:26:51,081 --> 00:26:53,000
The ingenious concept of a mobile hoist

466
00:26:53,001 --> 00:26:57,160
revolutionized boat handling, outmoating the dry dock,

467
00:26:57,161 --> 00:27:01,240
the traditional method of maritime repair.

468
00:27:01,241 --> 00:27:04,420
But no one could have foreseen the $3 million giant in use

469
00:27:04,421 --> 00:27:07,660
today in San Diego Harbor.

470
00:27:07,661 --> 00:27:11,480
Just getting it assembled was a major logistical effort.

471
00:27:11,481 --> 00:27:14,220
The machine was built by Marine Travel Lift in Sturgeon Bay,

472
00:27:14,320 --> 00:27:18,720
Wisconsin, and came across country on 19 semis, piece

473
00:27:18,820 --> 00:27:19,780
by piece.

474
00:27:19,880 --> 00:27:23,860
And then we brought in 275-ton cranes

475
00:27:23,960 --> 00:27:25,420
and started the assembly process,

476
00:27:25,520 --> 00:27:28,900
which ended up taking a little under four weeks.

477
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:31,900
This Marine Travel Lift has transported dozens of boats

478
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:35,340
at up to $10,000 a lift.

479
00:27:35,440 --> 00:27:37,880
But few have tested its mettle.

480
00:27:37,980 --> 00:27:40,040
Then it got a big surprise.

481
00:27:40,041 --> 00:27:44,900
This boat was launched in the early 70s as a replica vessel to be used for training purposes.

482
00:27:44,901 --> 00:27:50,540
It got purchased by 20th Century Fox to film the movie Master and Commander,

483
00:27:50,680 --> 00:27:52,220
Far Side of the World with Russell Crowe.

484
00:27:52,740 --> 00:27:57,220
And then they sold it to the San Diego Maritime Museum about three years ago.

485
00:27:57,840 --> 00:28:03,220
Well, she's tipping the scales right now at 1,062,000 pounds.

486
00:28:03,440 --> 00:28:05,380
So she's about 530 tons.

487
00:28:05,381 --> 00:28:08,880
After moving the lift into place,

488
00:28:08,980 --> 00:28:12,180
Mario and his team begin linking the heavy nylon straps

489
00:28:12,280 --> 00:28:17,120
beneath the boat at critical stress points along the hull.

490
00:28:17,220 --> 00:28:19,220
The ship's captain watches anxiously.

491
00:28:19,320 --> 00:28:22,140
Well, any wooden boat, you've got to be very careful of them

492
00:28:22,240 --> 00:28:25,340
because there are so many pieces held together

493
00:28:25,440 --> 00:28:27,540
by so many smaller pieces, and just

